    "## 3. Three ways to mark up the same ticket\n",
    "\n",
    "### What and why\n",
    "Section 2 used XML-style tags, but a **delimiter** can also be a Markdown code fence or a JSON string. All three draw the line between your instructions and the customer's words. They differ in how many tokens they add, how easy they are to read, and how a customer could break out of them.\n",
    "\n",
    "Nothing here calls the model. The last part of the cell tries to break out of the tags on purpose, and shows how **tag escaping** stops it.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The idea: the same letter in three envelopes\n",
    "```\n",
    " XML-style tags             Markdown fence               JSON\n",
    " ──────────────────         ──────────────────────       ──────────────────────────────────\n",
    " <ticket>                   ### Ticket                   {\"ticket\": \"The auto-reply ...\n",
    " The auto-reply ...         ```text                       labelled my earlier message\n",
    " </ticket>                  The auto-reply ...            \\\"other\\\". It is ...\"}\n",
    "                            ```\n",
    " breaks if the customer     breaks if the customer       quote marks are escaped (\\\"),\n",
    " types </ticket>            types ``` (three backticks)  so it is safe, but hard to read\n",
    "```\n",
    "**Analogy — the envelope.** The address on an envelope is not part of the letter. A good envelope has a **name** (so the system prompt can say \"the message inside `<ticket>` tags\"), a clear **end**, and a flap the customer cannot write on.\n",
    "\n",
    "The flap is the weak spot: if a customer types the closing marker, the envelope ends early and what follows looks like your territory. For tags, the fix is cheap: before the ticket goes in, our code changes the `<` of *our own* tag names (`ticket`, `example`, `examples`, `label`) into `&lt;`. The model still reads the text, but it no longer closes the tag. Anything else, such as `<not received>`, is left exactly as the customer wrote it.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What the next cell does\n",
    "1. **`ticket = TRAPS[0][0]`** takes the first trap ticket. `TRAPS` is a list of `(text, label)` pairs, so `[0]` is the first pair and the second `[0]` is its text.\n",
    "2. **`for name, style in STYLES.items():`** loops over a dictionary. `STYLES` maps a name (`\"plain\"`, `\"XML tags\"`, ...) to a function. `.items()` gives you both at once, so `name` is the text and `style` is the function.\n",
    "3. **`shown = style(ticket)`** calls that function: a dictionary can hold functions, so one loop tries four styles.\n",
    "4. **`prompt_size([...])`** counts characters and estimates tokens (about 4 characters per token, as in Session 15).\n",
    "5. **`f\"--- {name} ({size['chars']} ...\"`** is an **f-string**: the `f` before the quote lets you put Python values inside `{ }`.\n",
    "6. The last lines build a **nasty** ticket containing `</ticket>` and a fake instruction, and print it in the XML style."
   ]
  },
