    "## 5. The system prompt as a file\n",
    "\n",
    "### What and why\n",
    "A **system prompt** is a behaviour contract: what the assistant is, which labels exist, which house rules apply and in what order, how to treat the tagged text, and how to reply. It should not be a string buried in a notebook cell, where nobody can see who changed it or when.\n",
    "\n",
    "As a **prompt file** in your work repository, it gets what code already has: a **version** in a small `---` block at the top (the **front matter**), a history in Git, and a reviewer who sees the exact line that changed in a pull request. Your program loads it at runtime, and fails with a clear message if it is missing or empty.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The idea: the SOP binder, not a sticky note\n",
    "```\n",
    " prompts/support_classifier/system.md              load_prompt_file(path)            PromptFile\n",
    " ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐          ─────────────────────────         ────────────────────────\n",
    " │ ---                                  │  ──────►  1. file missing? clear error ──►  meta    {name, version}\n",
    " │ name: support_classifier             │           2. read text (ignore a BOM)       body    the prompt text\n",
    " │ version: 2                           │           3. sha256 → first 8 chars         sha     e.g. 3f9a1c07\n",
    " │ ---                                  │           4. split front matter / body      version \"2\"\n",
    " │ You classify messages for a bank ... │           5. body empty? clear error\n",
    " └──────────────────────────────────────┘\n",
    "```\n",
    "**Analogy — the SOP binder.** A new service-desk employee gets a printed binder with a label on the spine (the front matter) and a seal (the **fingerprint**: the first 8 characters of a sha256 hash of the file). If one word in the binder changes, the seal changes. A sticky note on a monitor has no label, no seal and no history.\n",
    "\n",
    "You will keep two files in `prompts/support_classifier/`:\n",
    "- **`system.md`** — the contract. It applies to every ticket and changes rarely, through review.\n",
    "- **`user.j2`** — the Jinja template for one ticket. It may use three variables only: `ticket`, `examples` and `labels`.\n",
    "\n",
    "They change for different reasons: the contract when the bank's rules change, the template when the layout changes.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What the next cell does\n",
    "1. **`WORK_REPO = Path(os.getenv(\"WORK_REPO\") or Path.home() / \"Documents\" / \"genai-course-work\")`** builds a path with **pathlib**. `Path.home()` is your user folder (`C:\\Users\\you` on Windows), and `/` joins folder names safely on every operating system. `os.getenv(\"WORK_REPO\")` reads the `WORK_REPO=` line from your `.env` first; only if it is not set does `or` fall back to `Documents\\genai-course-work`. Every session from here on (including Session 17) finds your work repository the same way, so it is set in ONE place. If your work repository is somewhere else, set `WORK_REPO` in `.env` rather than editing this line.\n",
    "2. **`write_starters(WORK_REPO)`** creates `system.md` and `user.j2` **only if they are missing**, with deliberately weak starter text. It never overwrites a file you have edited.\n",
    "3. **`[p.name for p in made] or \"nothing: ...\"`** is a **list comprehension**: \"for each path `p` in `made`, take `p.name`\". An empty list counts as false in Python, so `or` prints the message instead when nothing was created.\n",
    "4. **`load_prompt_file(folder / name)`** loads each file and prints its version (`f.version or '-'` shows `-` when there is none), its fingerprint, and its length.\n",
    "5. The last `try/except` asks for a file that does not exist, so you can see the **`PromptFileError`** message your program would show."
   ]
  },
