    "## 6. Few-shot examples from a labelled set\n",
    "\n",
    "### What and why\n",
    "**Few-shot examples** show the model what you mean: a ticket and its correct label, a few times, before the real ticket. They do not train the model (its weights do not change); they are part of the prompt, so they cost tokens on **every** call.\n",
    "\n",
    "The examples must come from somewhere. The bank has a **labelled set**: invented tickets with the correct label written by people. It is split **once**: `TRAIN` (32 tickets, 8 per label) is the **training pool** where examples come from; `HELDOUT` (20 tickets, 5 per label) is only ever used to *measure*. If a held-out ticket is used as an example, the test is marking its own homework. That is **leakage**, and the guard in this section refuses it.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The idea: practice papers and the real exam\n",
    "```\n",
    "            LABELLED SET (52 invented tickets, labelled by people)\n",
    "                                │  split ONCE, never mixed again\n",
    "              ┌─────────────────┴──────────────────┐\n",
    "              ▼                                    ▼\n",
    "   TRAIN · 32 tickets (8 per label)      HELDOUT · 20 tickets (5 per label)\n",
    "   pick_examples → K per label           score_files measures on these\n",
    "   goes INTO the prompt                  NEVER goes into a prompt\n",
    "              │                                    ▲\n",
    "              └──── check_no_leak: an example that is also here? → LeakError\n",
    "```\n",
    "**Analogy — the exam.** A teacher who hands out the exam paper as practice, then celebrates 100%, has learned nothing about the students. The usual way it sneaks in: you see a ticket the model got wrong, you paste it in as an example \"to fix it\", and you test again on the same ticket. Of course it passes now. **Fix a failing ticket by changing a rule, never by pasting the ticket in.**\n",
    "\n",
    "`pick_examples` is careful in three ways: it is **balanced** (the same number per label, so no label looks more common than it is), **repeatable** (the same examples every run, so a change in your score is caused by your change, not by luck), and it ends with `check_no_leak`.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What the next cell does\n",
    "1. **`print(GUIDE)`** shows the bank's labelling guide: the rules the people who labelled the data followed. Your `system.md` should carry these rules.\n",
    "2. **`sum(1 for _, l in TRAIN if l == lab_name)`** counts tickets per label. The part inside `sum(...)` is a **generator expression**: \"a 1 for every pair whose label matches\". `_` is the usual name for a value you do not need (here, the text). The loop variable is `lab_name`, not `lab`, so it does not overwrite the model `lab` from section 1.\n",
    "3. **`K = 2`** is yours to change (0 to 4): examples per label.\n",
    "4. **`pick_examples(k_per_label=K) if K else []`** uses the one-line if/else from section 1: no examples when `K` is 0.\n",
    "5. Prints the chosen examples. **`{e['label']:<7}`** pads the label to 7 characters so the texts line up.\n",
    "6. **Try to cheat:** wraps the first held-out ticket as an example (`to_examples` turns `(text, label)` pairs into `{'text': ..., 'label': ...}` dictionaries) and passes it to `check_no_leak`, which raises **`LeakError`**."
   ]
