    "## 7. Measure on tickets the prompt has never seen\n",
    "\n",
    "### What and why\n",
    "So far you have built the pieces. Now you find out whether they work. Two classifiers run on the **same twenty held-out tickets**:\n",
    "- the **baseline**: Session 14's one line of instruction, the ticket pasted in, nothing else;\n",
    "- the **structured** one: *your* `system.md`, *your* `user.j2`, and the `K` examples per label from section 6.\n",
    "\n",
    "The number you get is the **held-out accuracy**, the honest number. Because none of these twenty tickets is in any prompt, it tells you how the classifier is likely to do on tomorrow's tickets, not just on the ones it has already seen.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The idea: same exam, two candidates\n",
    "```\n",
    "                 20 HELDOUT tickets (never in a prompt)\n",
    "                    │                             │\n",
    "                    ▼                             ▼\n",
    "     baseline_messages(ticket)        build_messages(system.md, user.j2, ticket, examples)\n",
    "     one line, no tags, no rules      your contract + tags + K examples per label\n",
    "                    │                             │\n",
    "                    ▼  Lab.chat (cached)          ▼  Lab.chat (cached)\n",
    "               read the label                read the label\n",
    "                    │                             │\n",
    "                    ▼                             ▼\n",
    "             right / 20                     right / 20 · mistakes · confusion table\n",
    "```\n",
    "A fair comparison changes **one thing**: the prompt. Same tickets, same model, same way of reading the label.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then read the **mistakes**, not only the score. The **confusion table** puts what each ticket really was in the rows and what your classifier said in the columns. The diagonal is correct; anything off the diagonal shows *where* mistakes go. Five `account` tickets in the `other` column usually means one missing rule, not five separate problems.\n",
