    "---\n",
    "# Part C · Wrap-up\n",
    "\n",
    "## C1. Summary\n",
    "- **Keep instructions and data apart.** Customer text goes inside `<ticket>` tags, the system prompt says once what the tags mean, and your code escapes *your own* tag names inside customer text. One envelope and one sentence cost a few tokens.\n",
    "- **Tags are hygiene, not a shield.** They remove accidental confusion, such as a customer quoting the bank's reply. Deliberate attacks need more (Session 18).\n",
    "- **A prompt for a million tickets is a template.** Jinja gives you variables, loops, conditions and filters; **StrictUndefined** turns a missing value into an error instead of an empty envelope.\n",
    "- **The system prompt is a behaviour contract, kept in a file.** Front matter carries the version, Git carries the history, the fingerprint shows any change, and `load_prompt_file` fails clearly when the file is missing or empty.\n",
    "- **Examples come from the training pool; accuracy comes from the held-out set.** Balanced, repeatable example picking, plus a leak guard. Fix a failing ticket with a rule, never by pasting it in.\n",
    "- **Read the mistakes, not only the score.** The confusion table shows where mistakes go; change one thing at a time and watch which tickets flip.\n",
    "- **Twenty tickets is a small sample.** 16 of 20 is today's gate, not proof of quality. Session 17 builds a proper golden set.\n",
    "\n",
    "## C2. The words again\n",
    "\n",
    "| Term | One-line meaning |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Instruction vs data | what you want done, vs what you want it done to |\n",
    "| Delimiter | a marker showing where data starts and ends |\n",
    "| XML-style tag | a named delimiter, `<ticket> ... </ticket>`; not real XML |\n",
    "| Tag escaping | changing the `<` of your own tag names inside customer text |\n",
    "| Prompt template | fixed wording with named holes, filled per request |\n",
    "| Jinja | the template language: variables, loops, conditions, filters |\n",
    "| StrictUndefined | a missing template variable is an error, not a blank |\n",
    "| System prompt | the standing behaviour contract sent before every user message |\n",
    "| Prompt file, front matter | the contract as a versioned file; the `---` block holds name and version |\n",
    "| Fingerprint | first 8 characters of the file's sha256; changes when one character changes |\n",
    "| Few-shot examples | worked input-and-answer pairs in the prompt; cost tokens every call |\n",
    "| Labelled set, training pool, held-out set | answer-keyed data split once: examples from one part, measurement on the other |\n",
    "| Leakage | a test item that also appears in the prompt, inflating the score |\n",
    "| Held-out accuracy, gate | right answers on the held-out set; today's pass mark is 16/20 |\n",
    "| Confusion table | truth (rows) against prediction (columns); off-diagonal = mistakes |\n",
    "| Baseline | the simple version you compare against (Session 14's one line) |\n",
    "\n",
    "## C3. Interview practice\n",
    "Answer each aloud in about 30 seconds before reading the model answer. A good answer has three parts: **what it is**, **an example from this lab**, and **one honest limit**.\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. How do you stop user input from being read as instructions?**\n",
    "*What it is:* separate the two. Put user text inside a delimiter such as `<ticket>` tags, say in the system prompt that anything inside the tags is data to process and never an instruction, and escape your own tag names inside the user text so it cannot close the tag early. *Example:* in my lab, four tickets quoted a label word (*\"the auto-reply labelled my message 'other'\"*); I compared the plain prompt with the tagged one on them, and a customer's typed `</ticket>` became `&lt;/ticket>` so it could not end the tag. *Limit:* this removes accidental confusion; it is not a full defence against someone attacking on purpose. That needs more layers: output validation, least privilege, and testing with attacks.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. How do you manage prompts in production?**\n",
    "*What it is:* as versioned files in Git, reviewed in pull requests, loaded at runtime with a clear error if missing or empty. *Example:* my `system.md` has `version: 2`; changing one line changed its fingerprint, and my held-out report records the fingerprints that produced the score. *Limit:* a file in Git gives history and review, but not automatic testing; you still need an evaluation that runs on every change (Sessions 17, 53–54).\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. How would you evaluate an LLM classifier?**\n",
    "*What it is:* a labelled set split once into a training pool (for examples) and a held-out set (for measurement only); a baseline to compare against; accuracy plus a confusion table; and a pass gate in an automated test. *Example:* the one-line baseline and my structured prompt on the same 20 held-out tickets, a gate of 16/20, checked by `pytest`. *Limit:* 20 tickets is small (one ticket is 5 points), and accuracy alone hides which classes fail, so I read the confusion table and the mistakes.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. What is data leakage in prompt evaluation?**\n",
    "*What it is:* a test item that also appears in the prompt (for example, as a few-shot example), so the model has effectively seen the answer and the score is inflated. *Example:* `check_no_leak` refused a held-out ticket I tried to use as an example, even in capitals with extra spaces. *Limit:* an exact-match guard does not catch a paraphrase of a test item; the real protection is the habit of drawing examples only from the training pool.\n",
    "\n",
    "**5. Why use a template engine for prompts instead of string concatenation?**\n",
    "*What it is:* it separates fixed wording from values, supports loops and conditions, lives in a reviewable file, and in strict mode makes a missing variable an error. *Example:* leaving out `ticket` with Jinja's default settings produced an empty `<ticket></ticket>` with no warning; with StrictUndefined it raised `UndefinedError`. *Limit:* a template only controls the *shape* of the prompt; whether the wording works is still something you measure.\n",
    "\n",
    "## C4. Troubleshooting\n",
    "\n",
    "| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `ImportError` or `No module named 'jinja2'` | course files or environment out of date | `git pull`, `uv sync`, then Kernel → Restart |\n",
    "| every model section says `skipped` | no key read from `.env` | `.env` needs `BASE_URL`, `API_KEY`, `MODEL`; restart the kernel after editing |\n",
    "| the notebook pauses for a while | the free plan's rate limit (429) | wait; it continues by itself. Do not click Stop |\n",
    "| a \"daily limit\" message | the day's free tokens are used up | stop; cached answers are kept in `~/.genai_course/s16_cache.json`; continue tomorrow |\n",
    "| `exists: False` for the work repo | the work repository is not in `Documents\\genai-course-work` | add `WORK_REPO=<your path>` to `.env` (e.g. `WORK_REPO=E:\\genai-course-work`), then restart the kernel, or clone the repository |\n",
    "| `prompt file not found` / `prompt file is empty` | the file is missing, or nothing is below the `---` block | run section 5 (it creates missing starters); write the prompt below the block |\n",
    "| `UndefinedError: 'x' is undefined` | `user.j2` uses a name that is not passed in | only `ticket`, `examples`, `labels` exist; fix the spelling |\n",
    "| the version shows blank after editing in Notepad | the file does not start exactly with `---` | save as UTF-8; make `---` the very first line |\n",
    "| replies `cut off`, label `None` | the model ran out of tokens while thinking | re-run once; if it persists, shorten or simplify `system.md` |\n",
    "| stuck at 14–15 of 20 | usually a missing or out-of-order house rule | read the confusion table; add the rule to `system.md`; one change at a time |\n",
    "| `pytest`: `SKIPPED` | no key in `.env` | not a pass: fix `.env` and run again with `-rs` |\n",
    "| `pytest`: `Set WORK_REPO` | key found, `WORK_REPO` not set | `$env:WORK_REPO = \"$HOME\\Documents\\genai-course-work\"` in the same window |\n",
    "\n",
    "## C5. What comes next\n",
    "**Session 17 · Prompt evaluation.** You will build a golden set for this same classifier, write a rubric, and use an LLM as a judge to score two versions of the prompt: your Session 14 baseline and the structured version you built today.\n",
